## Of a [[person#Noun|person]]: [[attractive]] or [[pleasing#Adjective|pleasing]] to [[look at]]; [[beautiful#Adjective|beautiful]], [[handsome#Adjective|handsome]]; also, attractive but not [[particularly]] beautiful or handsome.
##: {{synonyms|en|pulchritudinous|Thesaurus:beautiful}}
##: {{antonyms|en|homely|uncomely|Thesaurus:ugly}}
##: {{ux|en|a '''comely''' woman}}
##* {{RQ:Milton Comus|page=3|passage=Not once perceive their foul disfigurement, / But boaſt themſelves more '''comely''' than before}}
##* {{RQ:Stanley History of Philosophy|volume=II|part=5|section={{w|Plato}}|chaptername=A Platonick Discourse, Written in Italian by [[w:Giovanni Pico della Mirandola|John Picus Earl of Mirandula]], in Explication of a Sonnet by Hieronimo Benivieni|subsection=3rd part of the discourse, scenes VI–VIII|page=116|passage=This is properly ''[[w:Venus (mythology)|Venus]]'', Beauty, vvhich kindles the fire of Love in Mankinde: {{...}} VVe ſee many perſons exact, and unaccuſtomable in every part, deſtitute of this grace and comlineſſe; others leſſe perfect in thoſe particular conditions, excellently graceful and '''comely'''; {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:South Twelve Sermons|volume=I|chapter=A Sermon Preached at the Cathedral-Church of St. Paul’s, November the 9th, 1662 [Julian calendar]|date=9 November 1662|page=55|passage=He that is '''comely''', when old and decrepit, ſurely was very beautiful, when he was young.}}
##* {{RQ:Pepys Diary|volume=IV|date=28 February 1664|page=58|passage=[T]he Bishop of London &#91;{{w|Humphrey Henchman}}&#93;, who sat there in a pew, made a purpose for him by the pulpitt, do give the last blessing to the congregation; which was, he being a '''comely''' old man, a very decent thing, methought. The Lieutenant of the Tower, Sir [[w:Sir John Robinson, 1st Baronet, of London|J[ohn] Robinson]], would needs have me by coach home with him, and sending word home to my house I did go and dine with him, his ordinary table being very good, and his lady [Anne Whitmore] a very high-carriaged but '''comely''' big woman; I was mightily pleased with her.}}
##* {{RQ:Prior Solomon|page=448|passage=Then had I come, preventing {{smallcaps|Sheba}}'s Queen, / To ſee the '''comelieſt''' of the Sons of Men; {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Wilde Dorian Gray|chapter=I|page=2|passage=As the painter looked at the gracious and '''comely''' form he had so skilfully mirrored in his art, a smile of pleasure passed across his face, and seemed about to linger there.}}
##* {{RQ:Burroughs Land That Time Forgot|part=II|chapter=IX|page=1143|column=2|passage=The chief's name was To-jo, and his household consisted of seven females and himself. These women were much more '''comely''', or rather less hideous than those of Tsa's people; one of them, even, was almost pretty, being less hairy and having a rather nice skin, with high coloring.}}
## Of a person, an [[action#Noun|action]], [[behavior|behaviour]], etc.: [[meet#Verb|meeting]] [[accepted#Adjective|accepted]] [[moral#Adjective|moral]] or [[social#Adjective|social]] [[norm#Noun|norms]]; [[appropriate#Adjective|appropriate]], [[becoming#Adjective|becoming]], [[proper#Adjective|proper]].
##: {{synonyms|en|agreeable|seemly|Thesaurus:suitable}}
##: {{antonyms|en|improper|inappropriate|unbecoming|uncomely|unseemly|Thesaurus:unsuitable}}
##* {{RQ:Hooker Laws|chapter=In the Seruice of Idols the Doores of Their Temples, the Sacrifices, the Altars, the Priests and the Supplicants that were Present Wore Garlands|section=65|page=344|passage=Doth any contumely vvhich vvee ſuſtaine for religions ſake pierce ſo deeply as {{...}} vvhen they {{...}} trample vnder foote almoſt vvhatſoeuer either vvee or the vvhole Church of God by the ſpace of ſo many ages haue beene accuſtomed vnto for the '''comlier''' and better exerciſe of our religion according to the ſoundeſt rules that vviſedome directed by the vvord of God and by long experience confirmed hath beene able vvith common aduice, vvith much deliberation and exceeding great diligence to comprehende; {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Coriolanus|act=IV|scene=vi|page=23|column=2|passage=This is a happier and more '''comely''' time, / Then vvhen theſe Fellovves ran about the ſtreets, / Crying Confuſion.}}
##* {{RQ:King James Version|Psalms|147|1|page=721|column=2|passage=Praiſe ye the {{smallcaps|Lord}}: for it ''is'' good to ſing praiſes vnto our God: for it ''is'' pleaſant, ''and'' praiſe is '''comely'''.}}
##* {{RQ:Burton Melancholy|chapter=Charity, Composed of All Three Kindes, Pleasant, Profitable, Honest|partition=3|section=1|member=3|page=527|passage=Behold hovv '''comely''' and good a thing it is for brethren to liue together in vnion: {{...}}|footer=From [[w:Psalms|Psalm]] 133:1 of the [[Bible]].}}
##* {{RQ:Homer Pope et al Odyssey|book=III|lines=503–504|page=128|passage=(A long proceſſion) timely marching home / In '''comely''' order to the regal dome.}}
